//
//  Secrets.xcconfig.example
//  WatchRank
//
//  Copy this file to `Secrets.xcconfig` (same directory) and paste in your own
//  token. `Secrets.xcconfig` is gitignored; this example file is committed.
//
//      cp WatchRank/Config/Secrets.xcconfig.example WatchRank/Config/Secrets.xcconfig
//
//  Get a token: https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api
//  Use the **API Read Access Token** (v4), not the v3 API key — the app sends
//  it as an Authorization: Bearer header so the credential never appears in a
//  URL, and therefore never in a log, a metric, or a crash report.
//
//  Do not wrap the value in quotes. Do not add a trailing comment on the same
//  line: xcconfig has no comment-to-end-of-line inside a value, so `// …` after
//  the token would become part of the token.
//

TMDB_READ_TOKEN = paste_your_tmdb_v4_read_access_token_here

//  Supabase — accounts and the social graph (roadmap Part III, Phase 20).
//
//  Create a project at https://supabase.com/dashboard, then Project Settings →
//  API. You need two values:
//
//    Project URL   -> SUPABASE_URL      (https://<ref>.supabase.co)
//    anon / public -> SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
//
//  Take the **anon** key, never `service_role`. service_role bypasses every
//  row-level security policy in the database; in a shipped app bundle it is a
//  full compromise of every user's data. The anon key is safe here by design —
//  it carries no privilege, and RLS is what protects the rows.
//
//  Same xcconfig rules as above: no quotes, and no trailing `// comment` on a
//  value line — xcconfig has no comment-to-end-of-line inside a value, so it
//  would become part of the key.
//
//  Leave both unset to build without social: the app comes up signed out and
//  the social surfaces report themselves unavailable.

SUPABASE_URL = paste_your_supabase_project_url_here
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY = paste_your_supabase_anon_key_here
